WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.360
The history of armor is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.640
a centuries-long arms race between&nbsp;
weapons and protective equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:13.000
From Roman segmented armor to medieval mail&nbsp;
hauberks and eventually to full plate armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.520
each generation developed new solutions in&nbsp;
response to weapons and tactics of its time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.320
But even these masterpieces of craftsmanship&nbsp;
would soon be challenged by a new technology.&nbsp;

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.920
This video follows the major turning points in&nbsp;
European armor history. We’re painting with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.480
broad brush, focusing on the innovations that&nbsp;
shaped the battlefield while leaving aside many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.760
regional variations and smaller developments.&nbsp;
This is the evolution of European armor.&nbsp;

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:49.040
Chapter 1: From Standardized&nbsp;
Roman Armor to a Status Symbol&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:55.120
The average legionary of the late Roman Republic&nbsp;
wore a helmet, a large shield, and a lorica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:02.000
hamata. These mail shirts consisted of up to&nbsp;
40,000 iron rings, typically weighed just under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:08.520
10 kilograms and offered excellent protection. To&nbsp;
make the mail as strong as possible, the ancient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:15.640
Romans alternated rows of solid rings punched from&nbsp;
sheet iron with rows of riveted or welded rings.&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.800
The lorica hamata remained in use until&nbsp;
the fall of the Western Roman Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:27.640
but during the first century CE it was replaced&nbsp;
as the standard form of armor by the lorica&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:36.080
segmentata—the famous segmented armor. Underneath,&nbsp;
legionaries usually wore a padded tunic, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:42.800
not only cushioned the impact of blows but also&nbsp;
provided a certain degree of comfort. This type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.120
of armor can be seen, for example, on Trajan’s&nbsp;
Column in Rome, which was completed in 113 CE.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.920
Segmented armor offered excellent protection&nbsp;
while allowing a high degree of mobility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:01.800
but it was also expensive because its production&nbsp;
was labor-intensive. Equipping tens of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:08.520
of legionaries with the lorica segmentata was&nbsp;
only possible through a highly centralized and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:14.560
standardized arms industry, such as the one&nbsp;
Rome maintained during the Imperial period.&nbsp;

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.360
In late antiquity, the Roman armies&nbsp;
grew ever larger, and soldiers were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.840
increasingly recruited locally. This made&nbsp;
it harder to equip the legions uniformly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.680
Cheaper armor was needed in vast quantities,&nbsp;
so by the end of the 3rd century, Roman forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:38.240
increasingly returned to simpler forms of&nbsp;
body armor that could be produced regionally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:45.440
In addition to the lorica squamata—a type&nbsp;
of scale armor used by many peoples since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.840
the time of the Assyrians—the lorica&nbsp;
hamata returned to widespread use.&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.200
From the late 4th century onward, many legionaries&nbsp;
increasingly fought without body armor altogether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:04.520
and relied solely on their shields for protection.&nbsp;
This was probably not simply a matter of laziness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:11.480
as the contemporary military theorist Vegetius&nbsp;
suggests , As the Western Roman Empire declined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:17.760
however, the centralized system that had supplied&nbsp;
its armies with armor also began to break down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:24.560
Armor workshops and large-scale metalworking&nbsp;
declined, and after the collapse of Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:30.840
rule in the West, armour went from being a&nbsp;
mass-produced item to a precious family heirloom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.680
and status symbol. Only the wealthiest could&nbsp;
afford a full set of body armor and a helmet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:43.480
so the trend toward fighting without armor&nbsp;
continued. The armies of the Germanic kingdoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.960
which were now competing for supremacy in&nbsp;
Western Europe, consisted of foot soldiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:54.800
whose equipment was hardly standardized, if at&nbsp;
all, and whose primary protection was the shield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.360
This did not change significantly until the 8th&nbsp;
century, when a new class of warriors emerged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.200
who could afford expensive armor: the heavily&nbsp;
armored cavalrymen of the Frankish Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:15.400
the Panzerreiter.
Chapter 2: From Shield to Mail&nbsp;

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.640
From the 6th to the 8th centuries, the&nbsp;
military focus shifted toward cavalry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.280
The Ostrogoths and the Franks, in particular,&nbsp;
increasingly relied on cavalry units,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.840
and Charlemagne ultimately created a professional,&nbsp;
carefully structured military system centered on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:38.720
heavily armored cavalrymen who used spears and&nbsp;
lances as shock weapons. They used the speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:45.520
their horses to increase the force of their lance&nbsp;
thrusts. Beginning in 805, every free man in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:54.640
Frankish Empire who owned more than 12 manses—that&nbsp;
is, approximately 160 hectares or 1.6 km2 of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.640 --> 00:05:00.760
land—was required to serve as a heavily armored&nbsp;
horseman equipped with mail or scale armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:07.800
a helmet, a shield, a spear, and two swords.
The price of this equipment was enormous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:14.360
Historian Jan Frans Verbruggen estimates that a&nbsp;
mail shirt cost about as much as a good horse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:22.920
and the complete equipment of a mounted warrior as&nbsp;
much as 23 oxen. Because of this immense expense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.360
and the intensive training required to fight&nbsp;
with a lance from horseback, armored cavalrymen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:35.040
had to be full-time soldiers. Most lived from&nbsp;
the land they owned, which was worked by others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.320
This gave rise to a warrior elite whose&nbsp;
members soon came to be known as knights.&nbsp;

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:47.800
In the centuries that followed, the basic design&nbsp;
of Western European armor changed very little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:54.840
as can be seen on the famous Bayeux Tapestry.&nbsp;
Mail shirts became longer, a mail coif was added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:00.480
shields changed shape, and helmets became more&nbsp;
conical, but the basic structure remained the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:06.960
same. The typical knight of the High Middle Ages&nbsp;
wore a helmet with a nose guard, a mail shirt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.160
that reached down to the thighs, and a large&nbsp;
teardrop-shaped shield that protected his legs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:18.080
He therefore looked quite different from how he&nbsp;
is often portrayed in movies and video games.&nbsp;

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:25.120
Over the course of the 12th century, the mail&nbsp;
shirt evolved into a full suit of mail armor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.200
Gloves, chausses to protect the legs, and shoes&nbsp;
made of mail were added, so that almost the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:40.280
body was covered in chainmail. Such armor is often&nbsp;
seen, for example, in illustrations of Crusaders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.680
Mail armor fit so tightly that it almost formed&nbsp;
a second skin of metal. It provided excellent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.920
protection against cutting weapons such as swords,&nbsp;
but offered little defense against blunt blows—for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:58.320
example, from maces or axes—which, while unable to&nbsp;
penetrate the mail, could still shatter the bones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:05.400
beneath it. For this reason, in the late Middle&nbsp;
Ages, a gambeson was often worn underneath—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:12.040
padded garment that distributed the force of&nbsp;
the impact. However, one vulnerability remained:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:21.800
pointed weapons could penetrate mail.
Chapter 3: From Mail to the Coat of Plates&nbsp;

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:27.320
During the reign of Henry II of England,&nbsp;
Gerald of Wales recorded the story of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:33.560
knight being pinned to his horse by an arrow. The&nbsp;
projectile supposedly pierced his mail chausses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:37.880
thigh, and wooden saddle before&nbsp;
penetrating the horse’s flank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.960
While it is unclear how literally&nbsp;
this dramatic account should be taken,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.880
mail armor was vulnerable to sufficiently powerful&nbsp;
arrows, crossbow bolts, and lance thrusts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:54.080
Because these weapons concentrated great force&nbsp;
on a very small point, they could burst or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.080 --> 00:08:00.280
force apart the riveted rings. This led to the&nbsp;
next stage in the development of body armor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:05.720
mail began to be reinforced with plates&nbsp;
made of horn, hardened leather, or metal.&nbsp;

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:12.680
After a long period of experimentation, the&nbsp;
coat of plates emerged in the 13th century—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:18.160
type of body armor consisting of iron plates&nbsp;
attached in an overlapping pattern to the inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.800
of a leather or linen outer garment. One of the&nbsp;
richest sources of information on this subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:29.880
comes from the famous mass graves of the Battle&nbsp;
of Visby in 1361, where 25 coats of plates were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:36.560
found alongside hundreds of mail shirts. The coat&nbsp;
of plates protected the chest, flanks, and back;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:43.400
it was usually worn over mail and combined with a&nbsp;
bascinet helmet. Gradually, plate-reinforced arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:49.640
and leg armor was also added. At the same time,&nbsp;
the characteristic great helm became widespread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:55.000
It was worn over the bascinet and&nbsp;
almost completely enclosed the head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:01.680
offering maximum protection but also restricting&nbsp;
vision. For this reason, it was worn only during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:07.840
lance charges and removed as soon as close&nbsp;
combat began. To prevent it from being lost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:12.920
it was attached to the armor with a chain and&nbsp;
hung at the man-at-arms’s hip when he took it off.&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:19.520
The combination of padding, mail, and plates&nbsp;
provided rigid protection that distributed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:25.000
energy of an impact over a larger area, allowing&nbsp;
it to resist even crossbow bolts and powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:31.240
lance thrusts. However, this layering also made&nbsp;
the coat of plates heavy, and there were still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:37.840
vulnerable spots at the overlaps and between&nbsp;
armor pieces—for example, the armpit areas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:44.200
This is precisely where attackers struck: They&nbsp;
used long, pointed weapons to thrust into the gaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:50.200
and maces, battle axes, and polearms such as&nbsp;
the halberd to deform or even pierce the armor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:56.600
Armorers responded by producing increasingly&nbsp;
resistant armor with more and larger metal plates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.840
The coat of plates slowly evolved into&nbsp;
plate armor. And if you like the historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:08.500
illustrations in this video, many of them have&nbsp;
been redrawn as detailed line art for our Military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:14.480
History Coloring Book—over 50 illustrations&nbsp;
from Roman legionaries to early modern soldiers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.680
You’ll find the link in the description.
Chapter 4: From the Coat of Plates to Plate Armor&nbsp;

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:29.440
In the 14th century, ironworkers were able to&nbsp;
produce larger quantities of high-quality metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:35.560
in blast furnaces. Steel, which could be&nbsp;
harder and more durable than ordinary iron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:40.360
became an increasingly important material&nbsp;
for armor. These metallurgical advances were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:45.800
accompanied by technical innovations. For example,&nbsp;
the development of sliding rivets made it possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:51.880
to construct articulated joint guards. Gradually,&nbsp;
this led to the development of full plate armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:57.160
which almost completely encased the wearer’s&nbsp;
body. This chapter of armor history is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:03.240
incredibly complex, and we are not experts in the&nbsp;
field. For a more detailed look at the subject,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:09.160
we recommend checking out Schola Gladiatoria&nbsp;
and Todd’s Workshop. Both Matt and Todd are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.160
highly knowledgeable about medieval armor and&nbsp;
have published numerous videos on the topic.&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:19.680
A complete suit of armor consisted&nbsp;
of a breastplate and backplate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:25.480
a neck guard, arm and leg guards,&nbsp;
gauntlets, and iron shoes known as sabatons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:31.040
A lance rest was attached to the breastplate,&nbsp;
allowing mounted warriors to brace their lance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:37.680
during an attack. Joints, the neck, and armpits&nbsp;
were protected with mail or articulated plates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:43.960
made of riveted metal strips to ensure mobility,&nbsp;
and the entire suit was held together by rivets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:50.880
buckles, and leather straps. Under it, a gambeson&nbsp;
was worn for padding, while a surcoat displaying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:57.360
the wearer’s colors or coat of arms was often worn&nbsp;
over it. A good example of what this looked like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:03.080
in the late 14th century can be seen on the tomb&nbsp;
slab of Edward of Woodstock, the Black Prince.&nbsp;

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:09.400
Plate armor offered such effective protection&nbsp;
that the shield—which had been a warrior’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:16.280
important defense for centuries—became less and&nbsp;
less important until it was eventually abandoned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:22.200
The combination of the bascinet and great&nbsp;
helm was also replaced by other helmet types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.640
first by the visored houndskull helmet and&nbsp;
then by the armet and the grand bascinet.&nbsp;

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:35.680
From the 14th through the 16th century, plate&nbsp;
armor underwent significant technological and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:40.680
stylistic development, which is why it is&nbsp;
inaccurate to speak of “plate armor” as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:46.320
single category. Various regional traditions and&nbsp;
influential centers of armor production emerged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.600
in places such as Milan and several cities in&nbsp;
the German-speaking lands: Augsburg, Nuremberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.600 --> 00:13:00.000
Landshut, and, somewhat later, Innsbruck. In the&nbsp;
15th century, two major styles of armor dominated:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.320
the Italian style, characterized by rounded,&nbsp;
smooth surfaces, and the German style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:11.240
which was more ornate and was distinguished by the&nbsp;
characteristic angular breastplate known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:17.520
Kastenbrust, from which Gothic armor eventually&nbsp;
developed. Gothic suits of armor were highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:24.120
refined examples of medieval craftsmanship,&nbsp;
combining functionality and aesthetics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:29.120
as can be seen, for example, in the works of&nbsp;
the influential armorer Lorenz Helmschmied.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:37.240
Among other things, he forged suits of armor for&nbsp;
Frederick III and Maximilian I. These suits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:44.160
armor were symbols of chivalry, power, and status,&nbsp;
and only the very wealthiest could afford them.&nbsp;

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:52.000
Although such plate armor weighed between 20&nbsp;
and 30 kg, it allowed for excellent mobility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:57.480
because it was carefully tailored to the wearer’s&nbsp;
body. The idea that it took at least four people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:03.000
to hoist a knight onto his horse is&nbsp;
therefore completely false. Running,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:09.600
lying down, and getting back up, or mounting a&nbsp;
horse—none of these posed a major problem.[RS1.1]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:16.520
Wearing armor became truly tiring only after&nbsp;
prolonged physical activity, because air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:22.160
circulated poorly beneath the tightly fitted&nbsp;
plates, making the armor hot and uncomfortable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:29.560
A second drawback was that it took up to half an&nbsp;
hour to put on the complex armor, and one often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:35.720
had to rely on help to do so. Once it was on,&nbsp;
however, plate armor offered the best possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.720 --> 00:14:41.880
protection against the weapons of the time—at&nbsp;
least until a new challenger entered the fray.&nbsp;

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:47.840
Chapter 5: From Plate Armor&nbsp;
to Pike and Shot Warfare&nbsp;

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:53.600
In the 16th century, large infantry formations&nbsp;
armed with firearms reshaped the battlefields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:59.640
of Europe. Arquebuses and muskets, combined&nbsp;
with pikes, became the most important weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:07.200
of European warfare—the age of pike and shot&nbsp;
had begun. Plate armor had to adapt once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.120
as existing armor offered insufficient protection&nbsp;
against the increasing penetrating power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:18.560
firearms. To counter this, around 1500,&nbsp;
armorers began hardening armor components&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:23.200
on a larger scale. Through complex heat&nbsp;
treatment processes, they were able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:28.400
produce plates that were significantly more&nbsp;
durable, though also extremely expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:35.160
However, as warfare also changed and ever-larger&nbsp;
armies with standardized equipment took to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:41.400
the battlefields, there was a need for large&nbsp;
quantities of affordable armor. Consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:47.440
a more cost-effective variant prevailed:&nbsp;
munition armor, produced in standardized forms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:54.280
for the majority of soldiers, often consisting of&nbsp;
thicker plates. Such armor was capable of stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.280 --> 00:16:00.040
bullets under certain conditions and was even&nbsp;
tested by firing at it to assess its quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:06.080
Because the armor became thicker and heavier to&nbsp;
keep pace with the increasing penetrating power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:12.320
of firearms, protection was increasingly&nbsp;
concentrated on the upper body. The armor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:18.520
was first reduced to a three-quarter harness and&nbsp;
then to a breastplate or cuirass with a helmet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:24.600
Nevertheless, the costs and disadvantages&nbsp;
of armor eventually outweighed its benefits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:30.480
Heavy cavalry continued to wear cuirasses well&nbsp;
into the early modern period, but the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:37.240
majority of infantry abandoned armor in favor&nbsp;
of agility, mobility, and tactical flexibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:43.520
What mattered now was no longer the protection&nbsp;
of the individual soldier, but the strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:49.320
of the formation as a whole. And if you’d like&nbsp;
to bring some of these soldiers to life yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:53.960
check out our Military History Coloring Book.&nbsp;
You’ll find it linked in the description.

